WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.345
Lesson 5.5.

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:05.540
Smart-contract risk & what audits don't prove.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.192
By the end, you'll be able to weigh contract

00:00:08.192 --> 00:00:11.138
risk honestly, and read an audit for exactly what

00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:14.085
it actually covers, not what its own name implies.

00:00:15.385 --> 00:00:16.675
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:18.512
The four common failure types

00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:21.199
behind most real smart-contract losses.

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:22.904
What an audit actually covers,

00:00:22.904 --> 00:00:24.854
and precisely what it doesn't.

00:00:24.854 --> 00:00:27.308
Bug bounties, and why their size matters

00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:29.464
relative to the value at stake.

00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:32.011
And a full worked example, reading a real

00:00:32.011 --> 00:00:35.044
protocol's real audit and bounty numbers together.

00:00:36.344 --> 00:00:38.079
The four common failure types.

00:00:39.379 --> 00:00:41.300
Here are the four failure types

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.941
behind most real smart-contract losses.

00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:46.617
Logic bugs, straightforward mistakes in how

00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:48.681
the code is actually written.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:52.103
Reentrancy, where a contract gets called back into, before

00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:55.466
it's finished updating its own state, letting an attacker

00:00:55.466 --> 00:00:58.666
repeat an action it should only have allowed once.

00:00:58.666 --> 00:01:01.194
Broken access control, where a function that

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.966
should be restricted simply isn't, properly.

00:01:03.966 --> 00:01:06.890
And economic exploits, where the code itself is

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.361
technically correct, but the incentives, or the oracles,

00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:12.676
underneath it can be exploited anyway.

00:01:13.976 --> 00:01:16.486
Worth naming the other two failure types

00:01:16.486 --> 00:01:19.975
specifically too, since reentrancy and economic exploits

00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:22.356
tend to get most of the attention.

00:01:22.356 --> 00:01:25.673
A logic bug is simply a straightforward mistake in

00:01:25.673 --> 00:01:29.444
the code itself, an off-by-one error, a wrong comparison,

00:01:29.444 --> 00:01:32.996
a case the developer genuinely didn't account for.

00:01:32.996 --> 00:01:35.582
Broken access control means a function that

00:01:35.582 --> 00:01:38.403
should have been restricted to specific callers

00:01:38.403 --> 00:01:41.166
simply wasn't, letting anyone call it directly.

00:01:42.466 --> 00:01:45.941
Worth being precise about that fourth category specifically,

00:01:45.941 --> 00:01:48.049
since it's the one most likely to be

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:50.571
missed by a purely code-focused review.

00:01:50.571 --> 00:01:54.145
An economic exploit doesn't require finding any bug in

00:01:54.145 --> 00:01:57.134
the code at all; the code can execute exactly

00:01:57.134 --> 00:02:00.578
as written, every single time, and still be drained,

00:02:00.578 --> 00:02:04.477
because the underlying incentives or price feeds it depends

00:02:04.477 --> 00:02:06.751
on were exploitable from the start.

00:02:08.051 --> 00:02:09.141
Quick check.

00:02:09.141 --> 00:02:10.896
What exactly is reentrancy?

00:02:15.316 --> 00:02:18.455
The answer: a contract being called back into before

00:02:18.455 --> 00:02:21.891
it's finished updating its own state, letting an attacker

00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:24.852
repeat an action it should only have allowed once.

00:02:26.152 --> 00:02:27.847
What an audit actually covers.

00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:31.188
Here's exactly what to check on any

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:33.642
audit, before trusting its name alone.

00:02:33.642 --> 00:02:37.161
Its scope: specifically which contracts it actually reviewed,

00:02:37.161 --> 00:02:39.732
not just the protocol's name in general.

00:02:39.732 --> 00:02:42.598
Its date: specifically whether it happened before

00:02:42.598 --> 00:02:44.732
or after any later code changes.

00:02:44.732 --> 00:02:47.327
The severity of whatever it actually found.

00:02:47.327 --> 00:02:50.793
And whether those specific findings were genuinely fixed

00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:53.407
afterward, not simply noted and left as-is.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.566
Worth being completely direct about the actual

00:02:57.566 --> 00:02:59.817
limit of what any audit can honestly

00:02:59.817 --> 00:03:02.129
claim, since the word itself tends to

00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:04.862
imply far more certainty than it should.

00:03:04.862 --> 00:03:07.179
An audit reviews specific code, at

00:03:07.179 --> 00:03:09.267
one specific point in time.

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:12.017
It says nothing at all about code deployed

00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:15.151
afterward, and it can genuinely miss an economic

00:03:15.151 --> 00:03:18.412
exploit entirely, if that exploit never required a

00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:20.267
code-level bug to begin with.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:22.657
Quick check.

00:03:22.657 --> 00:03:24.632
What does an audit actually not prove?

00:03:29.052 --> 00:03:31.445
The answer: that the code, any later

00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:33.903
changes to it, or the economic design

00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:36.037
underneath it, is genuinely safe.

00:03:37.337 --> 00:03:39.252
Bug bounties, and time at risk.

00:03:40.552 --> 00:03:43.323
Worth being precise about what a bug bounty

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:46.093
actually does, and why its size relative to

00:03:46.093 --> 00:03:49.227
the protocol's own value matters so directly.

00:03:49.227 --> 00:03:51.649
It pays hackers to responsibly report a

00:03:51.649 --> 00:03:55.522
vulnerability, instead of quietly exploiting it themselves.

00:03:55.522 --> 00:03:58.268
A large, genuinely active bounty is a good

00:03:58.268 --> 00:04:02.227
sign specifically because it makes responsible disclosure the

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:06.122
more financially attractive choice, compared with an exploit.

00:04:07.422 --> 00:04:10.596
The same $50,000, at very different scales.

00:04:11.895 --> 00:04:15.503
Here's that exact same fifty thousand dollar bounty, held

00:04:15.503 --> 00:04:20.418
fixed, but measured against six genuinely different total-value-locked scales,

00:04:20.418 --> 00:04:23.839
purely illustrative, to show why the ratio matters far

00:04:23.839 --> 00:04:26.130
more than the raw dollar figure.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.883
Against one million dollars of T.V.L., fifty thousand

00:04:29.883 --> 00:04:33.935
dollars is five percent, a genuinely strong incentive.

00:04:33.935 --> 00:04:36.330
Against one hundred million, it's down

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.595
to zero point zero five percent.

00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:41.232
And against the source material's own four

00:04:41.232 --> 00:04:43.807
hundred million, just zero point zero one

00:04:43.807 --> 00:04:46.015
two five percent, vanishingly small.

00:04:47.315 --> 00:04:50.881
Notice how quickly that ratio collapses, as the exact

00:04:50.881 --> 00:04:55.210
same bounty gets measured against genuinely larger protocols.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.614
A fifty thousand dollar bounty was never actually the

00:04:58.614 --> 00:05:02.648
fixed, meaningful number; it's only ever meaningful relative to

00:05:02.648 --> 00:05:06.431
what it's protecting, and it can look perfectly reasonable,

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:10.150
or nearly irrelevant, depending entirely on that one ratio.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.782
Here's how to actually check an audit's

00:05:13.782 --> 00:05:16.463
scope and date yourself, rather than trusting

00:05:16.463 --> 00:05:18.395
a badge or a headline claim.

00:05:18.395 --> 00:05:21.410
Open the audit report itself, and read its stated

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.630
scope section directly, for the exact contract addresses covered.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.754
Compare that report's own date against the protocol's

00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:32.351
own deployment history, for the currently live contracts.

00:05:32.351 --> 00:05:34.599
And if the two don't line up, treat the

00:05:34.599 --> 00:05:37.466
gap between them as genuinely unaudited, full stop.

00:05:38.766 --> 00:05:41.886
Here's why code that's safely held large value

00:05:41.886 --> 00:05:44.408
for a genuinely long time is itself a

00:05:44.408 --> 00:05:47.961
real, meaningful signal, separate from any audit.

00:05:47.961 --> 00:05:50.816
Every day that code sits there, holding real,

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:54.727
significant value, is another day skilled, motivated attackers

00:05:54.727 --> 00:05:57.520
have genuinely had the opportunity to find a

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.931
flaw in it, and haven't succeeded.

00:05:59.931 --> 00:06:03.097
That's not proof of safety either, but it's real,

00:06:03.097 --> 00:06:07.466
accumulated evidence, earned specifically by surviving real attempts.

00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:11.186
Worth being direct about how these three

00:06:11.186 --> 00:06:13.841
signals actually combine, since none of them

00:06:13.841 --> 00:06:16.561
alone is genuinely sufficient on its own.

00:06:16.561 --> 00:06:21.235
A recent, well-scoped audit, a meaningfully sized bounty relative

00:06:21.235 --> 00:06:24.847
to T.V.L., and real time safely holding real value

00:06:24.847 --> 00:06:28.829
together, are what actually build honest confidence.

00:06:28.829 --> 00:06:30.936
Any one of the three, missing or

00:06:30.936 --> 00:06:33.107
weak, is a real gap worth pricing

00:06:33.107 --> 00:06:35.214
in directly, not explaining away.

00:06:36.514 --> 00:06:37.519
Worked example.

00:06:38.819 --> 00:06:41.043
Here's Protocol X, exactly as the

00:06:41.043 --> 00:06:43.174
source material lays it out.

00:06:43.174 --> 00:06:44.999
It has two audits on record.

00:06:44.999 --> 00:06:47.924
But its latest upgrade, deployed just last month,

00:06:47.924 --> 00:06:50.974
isn't actually covered in either audit's scope.

00:06:50.974 --> 00:06:54.124
Its bug bounty sits at fifty thousand dollars.

00:06:54.124 --> 00:06:55.804
Its total value locked sits

00:06:55.804 --> 00:06:57.724
at four hundred million dollars.

00:06:59.024 --> 00:07:02.234
Here's what that bounty-to-value ratio actually looks

00:07:02.234 --> 00:07:05.269
like, precisely, once you compute it directly.

00:07:05.269 --> 00:07:09.754
Fifty thousand dollars, against four hundred million dollars, comes

00:07:09.754 --> 00:07:12.722
to just zero point zero one two five percent

00:07:12.722 --> 00:07:16.745
of total value locked, an extremely small incentive relative

00:07:16.745 --> 00:07:19.647
to what's genuinely at stake if a real flaw

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:21.824
exists in that unaudited upgrade.

00:07:23.124 --> 00:07:25.773
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:07:25.773 --> 00:07:28.484
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:07:28.484 --> 00:07:31.249
more than the specific numbers involved.

00:07:31.249 --> 00:07:35.016
The newest code, the part actually deployed most recently,

00:07:35.016 --> 00:07:38.910
is entirely unaudited, and the financial incentive to report

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.293
a flaw in it responsibly is genuinely tiny, relative

00:07:42.293 --> 00:07:45.996
to what a successful exploit against four hundred million

00:07:45.996 --> 00:07:48.039
dollars could actually be worth.

00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:51.955
Worth being concrete about what size down,

00:07:51.955 --> 00:07:54.450
or wait actually means here, rather than

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.244
leaving it as an abstract recommendation.

00:07:57.244 --> 00:08:00.208
Size down means genuinely reducing the position

00:08:00.208 --> 00:08:03.049
specifically in that unaudited upgrade, to an

00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.004
amount you'd be fully fine losing outright.

00:08:06.004 --> 00:08:08.991
Wait means holding off entirely, until a real

00:08:08.991 --> 00:08:12.302
audit actually covers that exact code, rather than

00:08:12.302 --> 00:08:15.679
assuming the earlier two audits somehow still apply.

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:18.069
Quick check.

00:08:18.069 --> 00:08:19.643
Why does it actually matter to

00:08:19.643 --> 00:08:20.964
check an audit's own date?

00:08:25.384 --> 00:08:28.173
The answer: any code changed after that audit

00:08:28.173 --> 00:08:30.659
happened was never reviewed by it at all.

00:08:31.959 --> 00:08:33.419
Here's your checklist.

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:36.743
Do it before trusting any contract's own audit claims.

00:08:36.743 --> 00:08:39.751
Confirm the audit's scope genuinely matches the

00:08:39.751 --> 00:08:42.508
currently deployed code, and its date comes

00:08:42.508 --> 00:08:44.563
after the last real upgrade.

00:08:44.563 --> 00:08:47.574
Confirm any critical or high-severity findings

00:08:47.574 --> 00:08:50.373
were actually resolved, not just noted.

00:08:50.373 --> 00:08:53.579
And check the bug bounty's actual size, specifically

00:08:53.579 --> 00:08:56.603
relative to the protocol's own total value locked.

00:08:57.903 --> 00:08:59.148
Let's recap.

00:08:59.148 --> 00:09:02.789
Four common failure types: logic bugs, reentrancy,

00:09:02.789 --> 00:09:06.359
broken access control, and economic exploits that

00:09:06.359 --> 00:09:08.373
need no code bug at all.

00:09:08.373 --> 00:09:10.524
An audit reviews specific code, at a

00:09:10.524 --> 00:09:13.315
specific time; check its scope, date, findings,

00:09:13.315 --> 00:09:15.243
and whether they were fixed.

00:09:15.243 --> 00:09:17.325
A bug bounty's size only matters

00:09:17.325 --> 00:09:20.023
relative to the value it's protecting.

00:09:20.023 --> 00:09:21.809
Time and value safely held is

00:09:21.809 --> 00:09:24.133
real evidence, though never proof.

00:09:24.133 --> 00:09:26.416
And Protocol X shows exactly why all

00:09:26.416 --> 00:09:29.007
of this matters together: two audits, but

00:09:29.007 --> 00:09:31.228
the newest code isn't in either one.

00:09:32.528 --> 00:09:36.011
Check the audit's scope and date, confirm findings were

00:09:36.011 --> 00:09:39.048
fixed, and check the bounty relative to TVL.

00:09:39.048 --> 00:09:42.136
Next up, Lesson 5.6: beyond Ethereum

00:09:42.136 --> 00:09:45.390
— Solana, Bitcoin and other ecosystems.
